  "platform": "Mega Drive",
  "summary": "Bowie leads a growing group through an explorable fantasy world and battles fought one character at a time.",
  "body": [
    "Shining Force II is Sonic! Software Planning’s tactical role-playing game for Mega Drive, first released in Japan on 1 October 1993. Bowie and his companions investigate a disturbance that grows into a struggle against the demon Zeon. Exploring towns and travelling between them gives way to turn-based battles on a square grid. The army includes characters with different abilities and personalities, and new recruits gradually broaden the group. Between engagements, the player can revisit places, look for hidden treasures and prepare the force for the next encounter.",
    "Each character’s turn allows movement followed by an action such as attacking, casting a spell or using an item. A knight, an archer and a healer reach different squares and contribute in different ways, so their positions matter before an attack begins. Experience makes the characters stronger, and promotion opens more powerful classes once they reach the required level. Some recruits have an alternative promotion linked to a hidden item. Building the army therefore involves both battlefield choices and discoveries made while exploring the world.",
    "Away from the grid, towns provide conversations, equipment and opportunities to learn more about the companions. The journey can return to earlier locations rather than closing each chapter behind the party, making the world feel connected across successive battles. Headquarters offer places to meet allies who are currently outside the active force. Motoaki Takenouchi composed the soundtrack for this mixture of travel and tactical encounters. The result is an adventure whose larger company remains visible even when only a selection of its members takes the field."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Bowie leaves Granseal",
    "One square, one decision",
    "The people between battles"
  ],
